{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Then Israel sang this song.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the main subject of this song is the slain of the Amorites who were found in the wadi of Arnon, whose blood became mixed with the water of the well; and from this it mentioned above 'ואת והב בסופה ואת הנחלים ארנון.' And the explanation of the verse, in my opinion, is 'על כן יאמר בספר מלחמות ה' — therefore it will be said in the book of the wars of YHWH' — included in the reckoning of the war of YHWH, and בספר is from the root meaning counting (ספירה), that is: whoever counts the wars of YHWH and the great and mighty wonders that were done for Israel should include in his reckoning 'ואת והב בסופה' — that the Holy One, blessed be He, destroyed the city whose name was והב in a storm (סופה), 'ואת הנחלים אשר לארנון — and the wadis that belong to Arnon.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted 'ואת והב בסופה' as what He gave them at the Sea of Reeds (ים סוף), and 'ואת הנחלים ארנון' as what He wondrously did for them in the wadis of Arnon, for great miracles were also performed there. And what are those miracles? 'ואשד הנחלים' — the pouring of the wadis was blood; and this is what Onkelos translated (Leviticus 4:12) 'אל שפך הדשן' — 'to a pouring of ashes' — as 'מישד קיטמא.' The intent is that the blood of the Amorites who were hiding there was poured out, for the mountains were great and the wadi was deep and narrow, and the mountains were close to each other — a person standing on this side could speak with his fellow on the other side, and the road passed through the wadi. The Amorites said: when Israel enters the wadi to pass through, we will come out of the caves and kill them with arrows and catapult stones. When Israel came to pass through, the mountain shuddered over the Amorites like a maidservant going out to meet her mistress, and the mountains drew near to each other, and those caves entered those clefts and killed them, and the entire population perished just as the population of the Sea of Reefs perished. Therefore Scripture juxtaposed 'ואת והב בסופה ואת הנחלים ארנון,' and this is what it says: 'אשר נטה' — that is, that the rock leaned from its place and drew near to the side of Moab and adhered." "<b>And from there the well.</b> From there the flow came to the well — that is the rock mentioned above — and the well was bringing up the blood of the slain, and Israel saw this vengeance and how the Holy One, blessed be He, fought on their behalf and slew their enemies, while they themselves knew nothing of it until this well informed them, and immediately they sang a song. And what song did they say? 'עלי באר ענו לה — Spring up, O well, sing to it.' And the language of the song continues until the end of the parashah, until 'the wilderness (הישימון).'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:45.674196745Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}